                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="940-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Pearl Microphone Laboratory CB 22</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Bidirectional Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The CB 22 is a FET condenser microphone with Pearl&#8217;s <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/model-2800">Model 2800</a> rectangular dual-diaphragm capsule, configured  for a figure-of-8 pickup pattern.</p><p>The Model 2800 capsule uses a thin polyester diaphragm measuring 14x29mm, on a 17x35mm rectangular frame. Read more about the characteristics of <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/rectangular-diaphragm">rectangular condenser capsules</a>.</p><p>Although the mic&#8217;s advertised frequency response is 20Hz&ndash;25kHz, the -3dB points are closer to 45Hz and 14kHz.</p><p>The microphone name is engraved into the body, and painted pink to distinguish the CB22 from the CC22 (gold) and CO22 (white).</p><p>The CB22 can be paired with the cardioid CC22 for Mid-Side stereo applications, or with a second CB22 for Blumlein recording.</p><p>The mic ships in a padded aluminum flight case (p/n AC01). Two models of shockmount are available separately:</p><ul><li>Model 1927 - essentially a rubber-lined mic clip</li><li>Model 1928 - a traditional elastic shockmount</li></ul><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Pearl/CB-22">Pearl Microphone Laboratory CB 22</a> is also known as: CB22.</p>
